 "base_ref": "v1.20.0",
 "base_commit": "2e3fe965c963d45ae156c32ef230c7eb7a29a777",
 "issue": 1653,
 "issue_title": "Bug: validate scalar column values in from_dict",
 "issue_body": "## Summary\n`arnio.from_dict()` does not validate whether column values are sequences before delegating to pandas. This creates inconsistent behavior: all-scalar input raises a raw pandas error, while mixed scalar/list input silently broadcasts scalar values.\n\n## Why it matters\n`from_dict()` is a public constructor and its docstring describes a dictionary of column names to lists of data. Silent scalar broadcasting can hide malformed input, while the all-scalar case leaks pandas implementation wording instead of Arnio\u2019s API contract.\n\n## Evidence\n- `arnio/convert.py`: `from_dict()` validates the outer object and key types, then calls `pd.DataFrame(data)` directly.\n- The same function only rejects nested `dict` values before pandas construction; scalar values are not validated.\n- Runtime behavior on current source:\n\n```python\nimport arnio as ar\n\nar.from_dict({\"a\": 1})\n# ValueError: If using all scalar values, you must pass an index\n\nar.from_dict({\"a\": [1, 2], \"b\": 3})\n# succeeds and broadcasts b to [3, 3]\n```\n\n## Reproduction or trigger\n1. Call `ar.from_dict({\"a\": 1})`.\n2. Call `ar.from_dict({\"a\": [1, 2], \"b\": 3})`.\n3. Compare the inconsistent handling of scalar column values.\n\n## Expected behavior\n`from_dict()` should enforce a clear contract. If scalar column values are unsupported, reject them consistently with an Arnio-owned `TypeError` or `ValueError`. If scalar broadcasting is intentionally supported, document it and handle all-scalar input explicitly.\n\n## Actual behavior\nAll-scalar input leaks pandas\u2019 raw error, while mixed scalar/list input succeeds through pandas broadcasting.\n\n## Suggested direction\nAdd validation before `pd.DataFrame(data)`:\n\n- require each value to be a supported sequence-like column, or intentionally document and implement scalar broadcasting\n- reject bare strings as scalar values unless explicitly supported\n- add tests for all-scalar, mixed scalar/list, tuple values, and bare string values\n\n## References\n- `arnio/convert.py`\n- `tests/test_convert.py`\r\n",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/1653",
 "pr": 2151,
 "pr_title": "fix(convert): validate scalar values in from_dict",
 "pr_body": "## Summary\r\n\r\nValidate column values in from_dict() before delegating to pandas.\r\n\r\nThis change rejects unsupported scalar column values and bare string/bytes values with an Arnio-owned TypeError, preventing inconsistent pandas behaviour such as scalar broadcasting and raw pandas exceptions. Valid sequence inputs such as lists and tuples continue to work unchanged.\r\n\r\n## Linked issue\r\n\r\nFixes #1653\r\n\r\n## Type of change\r\n\r\n [x] Bug fix\r\n [x] Tests\r\n\r\n## Area\r\n\r\n [x] Python API / ArFrame\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n\r\n [x] python -m compileall arnio tests\r\n [x] python -m ruff check arnio/convert.py tests/test_convert.py\r\n\r\nAdded focused regression tests covering:\r\n\r\n all-scalar input\r\n mixed scalar/list input\r\n valid tuple values\r\n bare string values\r\n bare bytes values\r\n\r\n## Contributor checklist\r\n\r\n [x] I kept the PR focused on one issue or one logical change.\r\n [x] I added or updated tests for behaviour changes.\r\n [x] My PR title uses Conventional Commits.",
 "fix_commit": "af8878a33455c3827dd02a52037f2efc4041fb98",
